---
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

// The surfaces table is imported from the repository threat model so the public page
// cannot drift from the document engineers maintain. The path is anchored to the site
// root (`astro build` runs from `site/`), not to this file, which is bundled elsewhere.
const threatModel = readFileSync(new URL("../docs/security/threat-model.md", `file://${process.cwd()}/`), "utf8");
const surfaces = threatModel.split(/^## Surfaces\s*$/m)[1]?.split(/^## /m)[0] ?? "";
const rows = surfaces
  .split("\n")
  .map((l) => l.trim())
  .filter((l) => l.startsWith("|"))
  .map((l) => l.slice(1, -1).split("|").map((c) => c.trim()));
const header = rows[0] ?? [];
const body = rows.slice(2);
if (header.length < 2 || body.length < 5) {
  throw new Error("threat-model.md Surfaces table not found or too short");
}

function escapeHtml(s: string): string {
  return s.replace(/&/g, "&amp;").replace(/</g, "&lt;").replace(/>/g, "&gt;").replace(/"/g, "&quot;");
}

function inline(md: string): string {
  return escapeHtml(md)
    .replace(/`([^`]+)`/g, "<code>$1</code>")
    .replace(/\*\*([^*]+)\*\*/g, "<strong>$1</strong>");
}

const GH = "https://github.com/naffis/botpasses/blob/dev";
const adrs = [
  { href: `${GH}/docs/adr/0006-grant-vault-trust-model.md`, label: "ADR 0006: Grant-vault, not zero-knowledge" },
  { href: `${GH}/docs/adr/0007-kms-wrapped-kek.md`, label: "ADR 0007: KMS-wrapped platform key" },
  { href: `${GH}/docs/adr/0008-security-hardening.md`, label: "ADR 0008: Security hardening after the 2026-09 audit" },
  { href: `${GH}/docs/adr/0003-first-party-operator-identity.md`, label: "ADR 0003b: First-party operator identity" },
  { href: `${GH}/docs/adr/0004-same-origin-oauth-as.md`, label: "ADR 0004b: Same-origin OAuth authorization server" },
  { href: `${GH}/docs/adr/0005-access-ledger.md`, label: "ADR 0005: Access ledger" },
  { href: `${GH}/docs/security/threat-model.md`, label: "Threat model (source of the table above)" },
];
---
<Base
  title="Security"
  description="How Botpasses keeps credentials out of the model: the trust model and who can decrypt, encryption and KMS, connector hardening, OAuth, the access ledger, canary tests, and how to report a vulnerability."
>
  <h1>Security</h1>
  <p>
    Botpasses is a grant-vault. Credential values stay inside the vault process and are decrypted only
    at an approved <strong>inject</strong>: the moment Botpasses attaches the value to an outbound API call,
    hands it to a trusted runtime over <code>POST /runtime/resolve</code>, or (on the local CLI) places it
    in a child process environment. No MCP tool, HTTP route, console screen, or email returns a value.
    There is no <code>get_secret</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="trust-model">Trust model</h2>
  <p>
    This is not zero-knowledge. To attach a key to a request the hosted process has to decrypt it, so the
    hosted process (and anyone holding both the AWS KMS role and the database) can read values. The model,
    the transcript, logs, and staff without both of those cannot. We publish that instead of a claim we
    could not keep. Decision:
    <a href={adrs[0]?.href} rel="noopener">ADR 0006</a>.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table aria-label="Which surfaces see a secret value">
      <thead>
        <tr>{header.map((h) => <th scope="col" set:html={inline(h)} />)}</tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {body.map((r) => <tr>{r.map((c) => <td set:html={inline(c)} />)}</tr>)}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="encryption">Encryption</h2>
  <p>
    Each credential is encrypted with AES-256-GCM under a data key that belongs to its organisation, with the
    organisation id as additional authenticated data. Organisation keys are wrapped by a platform key. At
    boot, AWS KMS unwraps that platform key (<code>VAULT_KEK_WRAPPED</code>) through a role the Fly Machine
    assumes with Fly OIDC; the KMS key itself never leaves AWS and never sees a credential. A database dump
    alone decrypts nothing. Machine tokens are stored as SHA-256 hashes. The local CLI uses a master key you
    hold, with the credential name bound into the ciphertext.
  </p>

  <h2 id="connector">Connector hardening</h2>
  <p>When an agent calls <code>http_request</code>, the outbound request is constrained before the value is attached:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Exact host allowlist.</strong> The request host must equal one of the credential's allowed hosts. No wildcards, no subdomains.</li>
    <li><strong>No IP literals.</strong> Hosts are names. Botpasses resolves the name itself, rejects private, loopback, link-local, and other blocked ranges, and connects to the pinned address with the correct SNI, so a DNS rebind cannot redirect the call.</li>
    <li><strong>No redirects.</strong> A 3xx is returned to the agent, not followed.</li>
    <li><strong>10 second timeout and 256 KiB response cap.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Redaction.</strong> The credential value, its last four characters, and any access token Botpasses minted are replaced with <code>[redacted]</code> in the body before it reaches the model.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Every call is written to the audit log by agent name and credential name. Approvals are per agent and
    credential; use one-call (<code>prompt</code>) approvals for anything sensitive.
  </p>

  <h2 id="operator-identity">Operator identity</h2>
  <p>
    Accounts are first-party: an 8-digit email code plus a required authenticator app (TOTP) with backup
    codes. There is no password and no third-party identity vendor. Sessions are HttpOnly cookies;
    state-changing requests need a signed CSRF token. Operator actions require the authenticator step;
    a session that skipped it is refused with <code>mfa_required</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="oauth">OAuth for agents</h2>
  <p>
    botpasses.com is its own OAuth 2.1 authorization server for MCP clients. PKCE S256 is required; dynamic
    client registration accepts <code>https</code>, loopback, and desktop-app redirect schemes and rejects
    <code>javascript:</code>, <code>data:</code>, and <code>file:</code>. Access tokens are RS256 JWTs bound to
    <code>https://botpasses.com/mcp</code> with a 600 second lifetime and a unique <code>jti</code>; refresh
    tokens rotate. Clients that cannot do OAuth use a one-time bearer token issued in the console. Model
    tokens cannot call operator routes or resolve values; trusted runtime tokens cannot call MCP.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ledger">Access ledger and revoke</h2>
  <p>
    Every issued session, bearer token, and OAuth token is recorded before it is handed out, as a hash.
    The console Access panel lists who holds access, when it was first and last used, and which credential
    names were fetched. Revoking an agent marks its tokens denied so the next call is 401; revoking an
    approval stops one credential; revoking a session signs out one device. Revoke is operator-only. Nothing
    over MCP can approve or revoke.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tests">Tests that fail on a leak</h2>
  <p>
    The repository stores a canary value in tests and asserts that it never appears in an MCP tool result, a
    REST payload for a model, the inbox JSON, an email, a collect page, an audit row, or the SQLite file.
    Another test fails if the phrase "zero-knowledge" appears in the README or the source, since that is
    not a claim Botpasses can make. These run in CI on every push.
  </p>

  <h2 id="report">Reporting a vulnerability</h2>
  <p>
    Email <a href="mailto:security@botpasses.com">security@botpasses.com</a>. The same contact is published in
    <a href="/.well-known/security.txt"><code>/.well-known/security.txt</code></a>. What to include and what
    to expect: <a href="/docs/security/disclosure">Security disclosure</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="adrs">Decisions</h2>
  <ul>
    {adrs.map((a) => <li><a href={a.href} rel="noopener">{a.label}</a></li>)}
  </ul>
</Base>
